
  Nothing is configured here yet — no [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, no linter, no tests. This is the cheapest moment to decide, since
  it's all free before code exists and annoying to retrofit after. Note that dev tooling doesn't compromise the
  no-build-step decision: the app still ships as plain static files with zero runtime dependencies; these only run on
  your machine.

  - [1mLinting + formatting.[0m I'd use Biome — one binary that does both, fast, minimal config, one dev dependency instead of
    the four that ESLint + Prettier drags in. Worth it even solo: it ends all whitespace and style debate and catches
    the unused-variable and shadowing class of bug for free.
  - [1mUnit tests.[0m Strongly yes, and I'd use Node's built-in [38;5;153mnode:test[39m with [38;5;153mnode --test[39m — [1mzero dependencies[0m, no config, no
    bundler integration. This is the one I'd argue hardest for, because the entire module split in section 2 was
    designed around testability: [38;5;153mreminders.js[39m is a pure function and [38;5;153mstore.js[39m takes injected storage precisely so they
    can be tested without a browser. If we don't write the tests, that design bought us nothing and we should have
    written something simpler.
  - [1mEnd-to-end tests.[0m Playwright can genuinely do what unit tests can't here — grant notification permission, assert a
    real notification fired, verify [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m survives reload. But it downloads browser binaries and is substantial
    infrastructure for a five-file app. I lean [1mno for now[0m; the permission flow is the one thing it would really earn its
    keep on, and that's a reasonable thing to add later if that flow gives trouble.
  - [1mFuzz / mutation testing.[0m No. There's no parser, no untrusted input, and no algorithmic surface that would repay it.

  My recommendation is the first two.
